CLAIM EXTRACTION / INFLATION MAP
================================
Target: Mann, M.E., Bradley, R.S. & Hughes, M.K. (1998). Global-scale temperature patterns and climate forcing over the past six centuries. Nature 392, 779–787. (MBH98 / “hockey stick” paper)
URL: https://www.meteo.psu.edu/holocene/public_html/shared/articles/mbh98.pdf
Document type: academic paper
Date of map: 2026-07-30

-----------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT WAS LITERALLY MEASURED
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Spatially resolved reconstructions of annual surface temperature patterns (including Northern Hemisphere and global means) for AD 1400–1995, produced by multivariate calibration of a multiproxy network against instrumental data.

- Instrumental training data: monthly land-air and sea-surface temperature grid-point series (primarily 1902–1995; verification subset 1854–1901 and longer individual series).
- Proxy network: widely distributed high-resolution annual indicators (dendroclimatic, ice-core δ¹⁸O and accumulation, ice-melt, coral, and long historical records). Full network ~112 indicators back to 1820; progressively fewer indicators further back (57 back to 1600, 24 back to 1450, 22 back to 1400). Certain dense regional dendroclimatic sets were reduced to leading principal components.
- Method: Principal component analysis (PCA) of the instrumental temperature field to obtain dominant empirical orthogonal functions (EOFs) and principal components (PCs). Proxies calibrated against the time series of these PCs during the mutual overlap period via least-squares / SVD. Pre-instrumental PCs then estimated from the proxy network alone and expanded into spatial temperature fields.
- Target quantities: annual mean surface temperature anomalies (relative to 1902–80 climatology), with primary emphasis on the Northern Hemisphere (NH) mean. Skill metrics (resolved variance “b”, verification correlations) reported for NH mean, global mean, NINO3, and multivariate field.
- Temporal reach and skill: Reconstruction terminated at AD 1400 because networks available before that date showed little skill even for the leading EOF. By AD 1400 only the first eigenvector is resolved (~40–50 % of NH variance in calibration/verification). Skill and number of resolved EOFs increase toward the present.

Verification state: ESTABLISHED FROM SOURCE (methods, results, and supplementary network description).

-----------------------------------------------------------------
2. STRONGEST CLAIM THE SOURCE ITSELF MAKES
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Abstract (verbatim):
“Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperatures for three of the past eight years are warmer than any other year since (at least) AD 1400.”

Body (Results / temperature reconstructions; more quantified):
“Taking into account the uncertainties in our NH reconstruction \ldots it appears that the years 1990, 1995 and now 1997 \ldots each show anomalies that are greater than any other year back to 1400 at 3 standard errors, or roughly a 99.7 % level of certainty.”

Additional strong claim (forcing attribution):
Greenhouse gases “emerging as the dominant forcing during the twentieth century,” with solar irradiance and volcanic aerosols also contributing to variability of the past ~400 years.

Note: The abstract statement is the most widely quoted; the body statement supplies the explicit statistical threshold (3σ / ~99.7 %). The paper also states that almost all years before the twentieth century lie below the twentieth-century climatological mean and that the positive trend in the leading reconstructed principal component during the twentieth century is exceptional.

Verification state: ESTABLISHED FROM SOURCE (direct quotation).

-----------------------------------------------------------------
3. WEAKER DEFENSIBLE CLAIM THE EVIDENCE ACTUALLY SUPPORTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Under the multiproxy climate-field-reconstruction method, the proxy network available back to AD 1400, the calibration/verification statistics reported, and the uncertainty estimates derived from the reconstruction residuals, the reconstructed Northern Hemisphere annual-mean temperatures for the calendar years 1990, 1995 and 1997 exceed the reconstructed values of every earlier year in the 1400–~1980 interval at approximately the 3-standard-error level of the reconstruction’s own uncertainty envelope.

Scope limits inherent in the measurement:
- Applies to the NH mean (areally weighted), not global mean or regional fields at that confidence level.
- Relies on the stationarity assumption that the leading patterns of variability in the twentieth-century instrumental period adequately represent those of the preceding six centuries.
- Skill for the NH mean is only moderate (40–50 % variance resolved) at the 1400 horizon; spatial patterns are not skillfully resolved that far back.
- Uncertainty bars widen substantially before ~1600–1700 because of network attrition.

This is a reconstruction-based statistical claim, not a direct instrumental observation for the pre-1902 period.

Verification state: RECONSTRUCTION FROM SOURCE (measurement + reported error model).

-----------------------------------------------------------------
4. BROADER CLAIM ATTACHED DOWNSTREAM
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Provenance discipline applied. No downstream text was user-supplied.

RETRIEVED (IPCC Third Assessment Report, Working Group I, 2001):
- Mann et al. (1998) is cited for the reconstruction of global patterns of annual surface temperature back to AD 1400 that possessed significant skill in independent cross-validation tests.
- The more expansive statements that “the 1990s [were] likely to have been the warmest decade and 1998 the warmest year \ldots during the past 1,000 years” appear in the TAR and are primarily attributed to the 1999 extension (Mann, Bradley & Hughes 1999 / MBH99) or to the collective MBH reconstructions. The 1000-year claim therefore rests on a successor paper.

RETRIEVED (contemporary press / institutional framing, 1998):
- University of Massachusetts and media coverage emphasized the abstract claim that recent years were warmer than any since (at least) AD 1400. Some headlines and secondary accounts loosely extended the uniqueness language toward “the millennium,” although the paper itself stops at 1400.

RECALLED (later secondary literature, assessments, and popular science):
The MBH98 reconstruction (often conflated with MBH99) became the iconic “hockey-stick” graph. Downstream restatements frequently present late-twentieth-century NH warmth as unprecedented over the last 600–1000 years, sometimes omitting the reconstruction’s explicit skill decline, the 3σ statistical framing, or the distinction between MBH98 (six centuries) and MBH99 (millennium). Exact citing sentences for the most inflated versions were not exhaustively retrieved here; they are therefore labeled RECALLED and do not anchor failure-location classification.

NONE FOUND (for exhaustive enumeration of every press release or textbook):
No claim is made that every possible downstream use was located. Searches covered IPCC TAR language, Wikipedia summary of reception, and major citation contexts; the pattern of association with millennial uniqueness is well-attested but not every instance was quoted.

Attribution check notes:
- Where a downstream text cites MBH98 for a full-millennium uniqueness claim that only MBH99 supports, the item is MISATTRIBUTED (citation confers authority the 1998 paper does not possess for the longer interval).
- Ordinary inflation (dropping “at least AD 1400,” the 3σ qualifier, or reconstruction caveats) is also present.

Verification state: MIXED — RETRIEVED for IPCC TAR and abstract-level media; RECALLED for broader secondary/popular restatements. Field 4 is therefore partially provisional for the most expansive millennial claims.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
5. WHERE THE INFLATION BEGINS
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Within-source component (not provisional):
- Abstract states the uniqueness claim without the 3σ / 99.7 % quantifier that appears in the body. This is mild prominence asymmetry (abstract vs. results), not a large leap.
- The paper itself is careful about the AD 1400 terminus and declining skill.

Downstream component (partially provisional):
- Primary inflation occurs when the six-century claim is restated as, or visually paired with, a millennial (1000-year) uniqueness claim while still citing or associating the 1998 paper.
- Secondary inflation: omission of the reconstruction’s uncertainty envelope, the moderate skill at the early end of the record, and the statistical threshold, turning a reconstruction-based 3σ exceedance into an unqualified “warmest years in 600/1000 years.”

Failure location classification:
- PAPER: minor (abstract omits the explicit 3σ language present in the body).
- CITATION / PR-MEDIA / ASSESSMENT: primary locus of inflation, especially the association of MBH98 with the full-millennium claim that belongs to MBH99 or later multi-reconstruction syntheses.
- MIXED overall.

Verification state: WITHIN-SOURCE component ESTABLISHED; DOWNSTREAM component PARTIALLY PROVISIONAL (exact citing sentences for the strongest millennial restatements not exhaustively quoted).

-----------------------------------------------------------------
6. WHICH CLAIM IS THIS AUDIT EVALUATING?
-----------------------------------------------------------------

The quantified claim, as stated in the body of MBH98, that the Northern Hemisphere annual-mean temperatures of 1990, 1995 and 1997 each exceed every reconstructed year back to AD 1400 at three standard errors (approximately 99.7 % level of certainty) under the multiproxy climate-field reconstruction method and network employed.

Rationale for selection (Rule 2 — prefer the most precisely quantified version):
The body statement attaches an explicit statistical threshold and names the specific years. This is more auditable for construct and internal validity than the abstract’s unquantified version. The audit therefore targets the source’s own strongest quantified claim rather than a downstream restatement.

